I have read the archive production and Mr. Voight's message and my assessme=
nt is that there is no defense on liability.<br />
<br />
Let me set out why, because I know the firm has had a different experience =
on other files. An architect who specifies an assembly that fails is defens=
ible when the record shows that somebody else made the decision, or that th=
e design team recommended a protection and was overruled, or that the failu=
re has a cause outside the specification. None of those three is present he=
re. The archive is complete, which means its silence is evidence rather tha=
n a gap. There is no directive because there was no directive.<br />
<br />
The remaining questions are quantum and comparative fault. I think the remo=
val and replacement number is soft and I think the installer's substrate pr=
eparation is worth pressing, which together may be worth a meaningful reduc=
tion. But we should be having a conversation about resolution, not about tr=
ial.<br />
<br />
Grant, I would set the reserve on that basis.